    "article_content_CXmqDG": {
      "type": "article__content",
      "blocks": {
        "text_CrfwFz": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "80ch",
            "richtext": "<p>h2 Pet allergies<\/p><p>p Do you have dog, cat or other pet allergies? Did you know that most pet allergies are not caused by pet hair, but by the dander your cat, dog, or other pet produces? Pet dander is made up of small flecks of skin shed by cats, dogs, rodents, birds and other animals with fur or feathers. Pet-related allergy triggers or allergens also come from proteins found in the pets’ saliva, urine, and feces. The obvious way to protect your indoor air quality is to not have a pet in your home, of course. But if going petless isn’t an option:<\/p><ul><li>Keep pets out of the bedroom of the allergy sufferer.<\/li><li>Keep pets off furniture, especially upholstery. <\/li><li>Keep pets away from carpeted areas. <\/li><li>Vacuum carpets and furniture to reduce the amount of dander.<\/li><\/ul>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
        "product_desc_image_hapr9i": {
          "type": "product_desc_image",
          "settings": {
